= Sybil attack =

== Overview ==
'''Sybil attack''' occurs when one adversarial entity creates or controls multiple identities and uses them to gain disproportionate influence in a system that treats those identities as independent participants.

Sybil attacks matter when a protocol relies on the apparent number or diversity of participants for voting, reputation, routing, resource allocation, consensus, moderation, or redundancy.

Pseudonymity alone is not a Sybil attack. The attack requires one controlling entity to exploit multiple identities as if they were independent.

Mitigations change the cost or evidentiary basis of identity: trusted certification, scarce-resource commitments, social-graph assumptions, rate limits, deposits, proof-of-personhood schemes, hardware roots, or other admission controls. Each introduces assumptions and tradeoffs.

== Boundaries ==
* Sybil attack ≠ pseudonymity
* multiple accounts ≠ attack unless used to exploit identity multiplicity
* Sybil resistance ≠ proof of one-human-one-identity in every design

